  "self_critique": "Overall assessment\n\nThe run found a modest improvement: 0.603609 versus 0.6018, about +0.0018 absolute. The useful result is the gauge-fixed BCE variant, but the evidence and search coverage are too limited to justify strong mechanistic conclusions or \u201cconverged\u201d as the stop reason.\n\nWhat the harness/policy did suboptimally\n\n- It used unsupported, inconsistent diagnoses. Nodes 001 and 002 asserted a peak at epoch 8 and continued training-loss decline, while node 003 stated that learning-curve telemetry was missing. The policy should not build causal hypotheses from unavailable evidence.\n- The search was narrow: only three proposals beyond baseline, with two centered on the same overfitting narrative. This is insufficient breadth for convergence.\n- Node 001 bundled architecture, loss, regularization, and recency changes. Even if it had won, attribution would have been weak. Calling this a \u201csix-arm ablation\u201d while reporting only one aggregate score is also inadequate.\n- Node 002 predicted a paired multi-seed gain of at least 0.002, but achieved roughly +0.0018. Accepting it as the best candidate is reasonable, but the journal should separately mark the quantitative hypothesis as missed.\n- Uncertainty reporting is unclear. The baseline lists sigma=0.0001, but no seed count, paired deltas, confidence interval, or uncertainty is shown for later nodes. The 0.0001 difference between nodes 002 and 003 is likely unresolved noise.\n- Node 003 introduced adversarial importance weighting for a temporal-shift diagnosis without first establishing measurable shift. Its expected +0.0005 gain was too close to likely noise to be an efficient experiment.\n- \u201cConverged\u201d is overstated. \u201cBudget exhausted\u201d or \u201cno confirmed improvement in the current local branch\u201d would better match the evidence.\n\nWhat I would change in the scaffold\n\n- Require an evidence field for every diagnosis, explicitly distinguishing observed telemetry from inference.\n- Record exact parent-to-child config diffs, per-seed results, paired deltas, mean, standard error, and confidence interval.\n- Separate candidate acceptance from hypothesis validation. A model may improve while its predicted magnitude or mechanism is falsified.\n- Disallow multi-component proposals unless they are followed by a factorial or one-change-at-a-time decomposition.\n- Use a staged evaluation policy: cheap seed screening, then paired multi-seed confirmation for improvements near the noise floor.\n- Require minimum branch diversity before convergence: loss/objective, regularization/early stopping, sampling, capacity, and optimization should each receive at least one controlled test.\n- Add sanity checks for objective/metric invariances. If primary is ranking-based, user-constant logit shifts may not affect inference ranking directly; the scaffold should articulate how centering changes gradients or optimization instead.\n\nWhat the next run should try first\n\nFirst, confirm node 002 against node 000 with identical data splits and at least 5 paired seeds. Report every paired delta and test whether the +0.0018 gain is stable.\n\nThen decompose the winning change:\n\n1. Baseline BCE.\n2. User-centered BCE only.\n3. Gauge-fixing regularizer only.\n4. Centering plus gauge fixing.\n5. A ranking-aligned objective such as BPR, with all other settings fixed.\n\nIf the gain survives, inspect whether it comes from better ranking, calibration, reduced user-bias variance, or altered gradient conditioning. If it does not survive multi-seed confirmation, return to the baseline and test evidence-backed early stopping or stronger regularization before attempting temporal weighting or larger architectural changes."
